# Changelog fragments

Each pull request writes its changelog entry into its own file here instead of
editing `## [Unreleased]` in `CHANGELOG.md`. Two pull requests that both edit
that section conflict as soon as one of them merges; two fragment files do not.

## Format

- File name: `<branch-slug>.<section>.md`, for example
  `fix-sync-lock.fixed.md`. The branch slug keeps names unique. It holds
  lowercase ASCII letters, digits, `.`, `_` and `-`, and starts with a
  letter or digit: `fix-über` or an uppercase letter stops the roll.
- `<section>` selects the heading the entry lands under:

  | Section      | Heading in `CHANGELOG.md`                         |
  |--------------|---------------------------------------------------|
  | `upgrading`  | `### Upgrading from X.Y` (the last minor release) |
  | `added`      | `### Added`                                       |
  | `changed`    | `### Changed`                                     |
  | `deprecated` | `### Deprecated`                                  |
  | `removed`    | `### Removed`                                     |
  | `fixed`      | `### Fixed`                                       |
  | `security`   | `### Security`                                    |

- Content: one or more top-level bullets (`-` and a space), written as they
  will appear in `CHANGELOG.md`. Indent every further line under its bullet,
  code fences included: text or a fence that starts in the first column after
  a blank line ends the list. A fence closes with the same run of backticks
  or tildes it opened with. No line starts with `#`, inside a fence or
  not; the file name selects the heading. A change that belongs under two
  headings gets two files.

Every file in this directory apart from this `README.md` must be a fragment
that follows these rules. Any other file, or a fragment that breaks a rule
(including a code fence left open), stops the release roll before it writes
anything.

## Release

The version bump runs `roll-changelog.py` from the skill-repo skill, version
4.3.0 or later; an older copy rolls without reading this directory, and the
fragments wait for the next release. Before it
moves `[Unreleased]` under the new version, it appends every fragment, in
file-name order, at the end of its heading, creates a missing heading in Keep a
Changelog order, and deletes the fragment. The bump commit carries those
deletions. With no fragments the roll works as it does without this directory:
it still moves the existing `[Unreleased]` entries under the new version.

Preview the roll from the repository root (Python 3.11 or later). It writes
nothing; `99.0.0` stands for any valid version:

```sh
curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/netresearch/skill-repo-skill/main/skills/skill-repo/scripts/roll-changelog.py \
  | python3 - CHANGELOG.md 99.0.0 --dry-run
```
